One-time setup on Windows.
1. Install Node.js LTS from nodejs.org. In a terminal run node -v and npm -v; Node must be version 20 or newer.
2. Install Git for Windows, then check it with git --version.
3. Install Visual Studio Code and these extensions: ESLint, Prettier, Error Lens, glTF Tools.
4. Create the folder C:\dev\chronoverse, open it in VS Code (File → Open Folder), then open the built-in terminal (Terminal → New Terminal).
5. Run git init.
6. Choose one AI coding agent that can read and write files and run terminal commands (for example Claude Code, Cursor, or GitHub Copilot in agent mode). The project does not depend on which one you use.
7. Create AGENTS.md (Section 2) and docs/BRIEF.md (Sections 3–7) before the first phase.
The rhythm for every phase.
1. Paste the phase prompt from Section 8.
2. Let the agent work. If it asks a question, answer in plain words.
3. Run the acceptance commands listed in the phase yourself — do not just trust the agent’s report.
4. Commit your progress: git add . then git commit -m "Phase N complete".
5. If something breaks, paste the red error text and write: “Fix this. Explain the cause in simple words and add a test so it cannot return.”
