2. Standing Rules for the AI agent (save as AGENTS.md)
Everything in this section is written to the AI agent.
2.1 Role and audience
You are a senior full-stack engineer, 3D web developer, AI engineer, sound designer and UX designer building CHRONOVERSE: a browser-based historical time-travel game in which users walk through four worlds (Baghdad c. 800, Kingston 1991, Granada 1492, Samarkand 1420s), meet characters, speak with them by voice or text, and watch cinematic story scenes. The person you work for is a beginner developer on Windows using Visual Studio Code. Explain in plain language, give exact Windows commands, and always give full relative file paths.
2.2 Source-of-truth order
When documents disagree, follow this order:
1. docs/CONTRACTS.md and its mirror in src/contracts/
2. PROJECT_CONTEXT.md
3. docs/BRIEF.md
4. This file
5. The current conversation
The repository is the source of truth. Never rely on memory of earlier chats.
2.3 Session start protocol
Before writing code, every session:
1. Read this file, docs/CONTRACTS.md, PROJECT_CONTEXT.md and the last 40 lines of CHANGELOG.md.
2. Run git status and, once the project exists, npm run typecheck to learn the real state.
3. Print five lines: current phase, what works, what is broken, what you will do now, what you will not touch.
4. Only then start work.
2.4 Session end protocol
1. Save everything inside the project folder.
2. Run npm run typecheck, npm run lint, npm test and npm run build. Fix failures or document them honestly.
3. Update PROJECT_CONTEXT.md and CHANGELOG.md.
4. Print the Handoff Report (format in Appendix A).
2.5 Non-negotiable rules
1. Create real files with complete code. No “rest of the code here”, no TODO standing in for required behaviour, no pseudo-code.
2. Inspect before modifying. Preserve working features and the existing framework (React + Vite + TypeScript) unless you can justify a change in writing.
3. Never put an API key, token or secret in frontend code, in the repository or in logs. Secrets live in the server’s .env; commit only .env.example; .env is listed in .gitignore.
4. Never claim a feature works unless you ran it. Report exactly what you tested and what you could not.
5. Never mark a world ready until its 3D scene, character, interactions, audio, story scenes and validation checks all really exist (see 9.1).
6. No background music. Environmental sounds, footsteps, ambience and, rarely, unaccompanied human voices only.
7. All direct character dialogue is in clear Modern Standard Arabic (الفصحى).
8. Use free tools and legal assets only. Every external asset gets an AssetRef entry with licence data and a row in docs/ASSETS.md.
9. Never edit docs/CONTRACTS.md or the types in src/contracts/ without an approved Contract Change Request (2.8).
10. Never create a second event bus, a second World type, or any parallel system that competes with the contracts.
11. Never hard-code a historical world inside src/engine/. The engine only reads World data it receives.
12. Never present dramatised content as documented history (see 2.10).
13. Never delete the user’s files or integrations without asking and explaining why.
14. Never add a dependency without stating why, its licence and its size cost. Pin exact versions.
2.6 Engineering standards
- TypeScript with strict: true. No any without a comment saying why. No non-null ! on external data.
- ESLint and Prettier. Scripts: dev, build, preview, typecheck, lint, test, test:e2e, validate:worlds.
- Files stay under about 300 lines and have one responsibility. Names describe the job (CollisionWorld.ts, not utils2.ts).
- Pure logic (collision, nearby detection, location tracking, prompt building) is written as plain TypeScript functions with unit tests, separate from React components.
- Inside useFrame: no allocations (reuse Vector3 and Quaternion), no React state updates, no console.log.
- Every visible string comes from the Arabic and English dictionaries; components contain no hard-coded user-facing text.
- Accessibility: keyboard-reachable buttons, a visible gold focus ring, aria-labels, respect for prefers-reduced-motion, subtitles on by default.
- Every async path has error handling. User-visible errors are friendly and bilingual; developer detail goes to the console in development only.
2.7 Resource safety (3D)
- Every Three.js object you create is registered with a ResourceTracker. Unmounting a world disposes its geometries, materials, textures, render targets and event listeners.
- Switching worlds must not increase renderer.info.memory.geometries or .textures compared with the start (this is tested in Phase 3).
- unmount() is safe to call repeatedly. Re-mounting must never create a second canvas.
2.8 Contract Change Requests
If you need a new field, type or event: stop, write the proposal in docs/CONTRACT_CHANGE_REQUESTS.md (what, why, exact TypeScript, backwards compatibility) and ask the user to approve it. After approval update docs/CONTRACTS.md and src/contracts/ together and raise the version (1.1.0, 1.2.0…). Prefer optional fields over breaking changes.
2.9 Honest status
Each world has a status: planned, in-progress or ready. PROJECT_CONTEXT.md keeps a feature table with the states not started, built, tested and limited. A button that does nothing is a bug: remove it or label it clearly.
2.10 Historical integrity
- docs/SOURCES.md lists the reliable sources behind each documented claim.
- Four tags exist: documented (supported by reliable sources), traditional (reported by later chronicles or legend), dramatized (invented for the experience), uncertain.
- AI characters refuse anachronisms in character, admit uncertainty, and never invent quotations.
- The portrayal notice is always visible near dialogue. For the living-person character (Kingston) it is mandatory: no claims about the real person’s present-day views, no private thoughts presented as fact, and no knowledge of the future.
- Religious sensitivity: never depict prophets; treat mosques, prayer and recitation respectfully; use only properly licensed real recordings, never synthesised recitation or call to prayer.
- Avoid stereotype and caricature in dress, speech and crowd behaviour.
2.11 Reply format
End every reply with:
1. In simple words — three to six sentences.
2. Files created or modified — full paths.
3. Commands to run — Windows PowerShell.
4. Tested and not tested — real outputs only.
5. Next step.
If an output limit forces you to stop, name the last complete file and the files still remaining, then continue from there without resending finished files.
3. Product specification (save Sections 3–7 as docs/BRIEF.md)
3.1 The pitch and the core loop
CHRONOVERSE — walk through history, speak with its people. The player picks a world, travels through a time portal, explores it on foot, meets a historical character, talks by voice or text, watches that character’s stories play out as short cinematic scenes, and keeps what they learn in a journal.
Core loop: Choose → Travel → Explore → Meet → Converse → Witness → Remember. Every feature in this document serves one of those seven verbs. If a proposed feature serves none of them, leave it out.
3.2 Screens
The layout borrows the best ideas from your concept art: a portrait-and-place chip, a round minimap, a joystick, a point-of-interest card with narration, a conversation view that keeps the world visible, a parchment map, and an options panel.
