4.7 World data validation (npm run validate:worlds)
This script runs before every build and in the end-to-end tests. It fails on:
1. Duplicate ids, or ids that are not kebab-case.
2. Broken references: a location listing a character or object that does not exist, a homeLocationId that does not exist, an object whose locationId does not exist, a primaryCharacterId that does not exist, a dialogue action pointing to a missing character.
3. A spawn point or location centre outside the scene bounds, or inside a solid collider.
4. A modelUrl, portraitUrl or audio file that is not listed in assets[], or an asset without licence data.
5. Any audio asset tagged or filed as music.
6. Empty or missing Arabic text, or Arabic fields that contain no Arabic letters.
7. A character whose portrayalNotice is empty, or a living-person character without the mandatory notice.
8. For a world marked ready: any placeholder model, any location without a soundscape layer, any story without subtitles, any character without a knowledge file.
5. Engine specification (Task 02 in detail)
The engine is a general, reusable 3D player for any valid World. It owns src/engine/ and nothing else.
5.1 Public API and lifecycle
- src/engine/index.ts exports createWorldRenderer(): WorldRenderer. The returned object implements the interface exactly as written in the contract: mount, loadWorld, teleportTo, setPaused, setCharacterSpeaking, unmount.
- createWorldRenderer() is cheap and synchronous. The heavy code (Three.js scene, loaders, audio) is loaded by a dynamic import() inside mount(), which keeps Foundation’s first page load small.
- State machine: idle → mounting → loading → ready ⇄ paused → disposed, plus error (the canvas stays alive so loadWorld can be retried).
- mount(container, world). Creates exactly one <canvas> in the container (a WeakMap from container to instance prevents duplicates), renders through R3F’s imperative createRoot(canvas) so the engine owns its own React root, adds a ResizeObserver that calls root.configure({ size }), adds an overlay element for touch controls, then calls the same code path as loadWorld(world).
- loadWorld(world). Validates the data (the same rules as 4.7, as a light runtime check), fades out, fully disposes the previous world, builds the new one, fades in and emits world:ready. Only one load runs at a time: a newer call aborts the older one through an AbortController and discards its resources. On failure emit world:error with a readable message and keep the engine usable.
- unmount(). Safe to call any number of times. It stops the loop, removes keyboard, pointer-lock, touch, resize and visibility listeners, unsubscribes from the event bus, disposes everything in the ResourceTracker, calls root.unmount(), renderer.dispose() and renderer.forceContextLoss(), closes the AudioContext, clears timers, and removes the canvas and overlay from the container.
- WebGL context loss. Handle webglcontextlost (call preventDefault) and webglcontextrestored (rebuild the current world). If the context does not return within five seconds, emit world:error.
- Tab visibility. When the tab is hidden, suspend rendering and audio; on return, resume unless the user had paused.
5.2 Asset loading
- A single AssetLoader aggregates progress across everything a world needs (weighted by file size when known) and drives the loading screen.
- It uses GLTFLoader with DRACOLoader and the meshopt decoder. Decoder files are served from public/, not from a third-party CDN.
- Reference-counted cache keyed by URL; retries twice with back-off; every request accepts an abort signal.
- Engine rule: load only URLs that come from the world data (assets[], modelUrl, portraitUrl). In development, warn about any URL that is not listed in assets[] or that has no licence data.
- Fallbacks: a character without a model gets a clearly flagged PlaceholderFigure; an object without a model gets a simple marker shape. Both are allowed for testing only, and the validator blocks them in a ready world.
5.3 Scene building
Pipeline: validate → ground → prefabs → scatter (instanced) → sky, fog and lights → colliders → character actors → object actors with pick volumes → soundscape → gl.compileAsync → first frame → world:ready.
- Prefab registry. Each prefab is a pure function that receives parameters and returns an Object3D plus its colliders. Adding a prefab never touches engine core. Starter set: wall, floor, column row, arch, dome, iwan (arched hall front), fountain, stairs, platform, water channel, gate, ring wall, tent, bookshelf, table.
- Materials. One shared material per preset and tint (sandstone, limestone, brick, plaster, timber, marble, blue tile, geometric tile, carpet, water, grass, cobble, dirt, iron), built on MeshStandardMaterial with CC0 textures at 1K from sources such as ambientCG or Poly Haven. Record each in docs/ASSETS.md.
- Merging. Static meshes are merged per material with BufferGeometryUtils.mergeGeometries before they are added. Colliders stay separate.
- Light. One directional sun with a shadow map of 2048 (desktop) or 1024 (mobile), its shadow frustum about 40 m wide, following the player and snapped to texel steps to avoid shimmer. Environment lighting from a small CC0 sky image (for example Poly Haven at 1K) or a hemisphere light. Fog from theme.fog; sky gradient from theme.skyTop and theme.skyBottom. Allow at most six real point lights, chosen by distance to the player; lanterns beyond that are emissive meshes with a soft sprite glow.
5.4 First-person player
- Dimensions (metres). Capsule radius 0.35, height 1.7, eye height 1.6, step height 0.35. Walk 3.2 m/s, sprint 5.5 m/s, acceleration 18 m/s², deceleration 24 m/s².
- Input abstraction. Keyboard and touch both produce a MoveIntent { x, z, sprint } and a LookDelta { dx, dy }. The controller reads only those, so new input devices never touch movement code.
- Camera. Store yaw and pitch as numbers in radians. Pitch is clamped to ±1.48 rad (about 85 degrees), yaw wraps to (−π, π], rotation order YXZ, no roll. Forward is −Z. The spawn Euler supplies the starting yaw and pitch.
- Mouse. Use the Pointer Lock API with movementX and movementY times a sensitivity (default 0.0022 rad per pixel). Fall back to drag-to-look when the lock is refused. Discard movement spikes larger than 200 px right after the lock is acquired.
- Fixed timestep. Accumulate frame time (clamped to 0.1 s) and step the simulation at 1/60 s so collision results never depend on frame rate. Interpolate the camera between steps.
- Pause and resume. setPaused(true) zeroes velocity, releases pointer lock, suspends audio and sets the render loop to never. setPaused(false) restores all of it. During dialogue or a story the player is frozen but the loop keeps running so animation continues.
- Bounds. Use scene.bounds when present; otherwise the union of all locations expanded by 30 m. With neither, run unbounded and log a development warning.
- Head bob. Optional, 0.025 m amplitude, off under reduced motion.
5.5 Collision system
CollisionWorld is plain TypeScript with no Three.js dependency, so it can be unit-tested quickly.
- Collider kinds. box (centre, size, yaw) and cylinder (centre, radius, height). Each has a vertical extent so floors and ceilings work, and an optional walkableTop for platforms and steps.
- Broad phase. A spatial hash with 4 m cells; query by the player’s bounding box; reuse arrays to avoid allocation.
- Horizontal resolution. Circle-versus-rotated-box and circle-versus-circle tests, push out along the smallest penetration, slide by removing the normal component of velocity, up to three iterations. Sub-step when speed × dt exceeds half the radius, so fast movement never tunnels through thin walls.
- Vertical resolution. Ground height is the highest of the base ground and any walkable top within step height of the feet. A step up of 0.35 m or less is taken; a larger rise blocks. Falls use 20 m/s² gravity with a terminal speed of 25 m/s and snap to the ground within 0.05 m. Ceilings block when the head would pass into them.
- Extensible. addCollider, removeCollider(id) and a small dynamic list (characters are cylinders of radius 0.4 so the player cannot walk through them).
- Any size of world. All units are metres and nothing assumes a world size. If bounds exceed 2 km, recentre the world around the player (floating origin) to protect floating-point precision.
- Debug. Shift+F3 toggles a wireframe collider overlay (development builds only).
- Unit tests required. Blocked by a wall; slide along a wall; corner; step up 0.3 m; blocked by a 0.5 m step; fall from 3 m and land; no tunnelling through a thin wall at high speed; NPC cylinder blocks; bounds clamp; teleport into a collider is pushed out.
5.6 Interaction system
- Pick volumes. Every interactable object and character has an invisible sphere or capsule on its own layer. Only that layer is raycast, never the full scene.
- Ray. From the screen centre along the camera’s forward direction, far limit 6 m, evaluated at 15 Hz (not every frame). When the pointer is unlocked (a card is open), use the mouse position for hover and click instead.
- Selection. A target is valid when its distance is within interactionRadius (3 m by default for characters). With several valid targets pick the smallest angle to the crosshair, then the nearest. Touch assist: if the ray hits nothing but exactly one valid target is within 1.2 m and 35 degrees of the view direction, focus it.
- Events. Emit object:focus only when focus changes. Show the object’s prompt in the current language. The reticle has three states: idle, focused (ring expands, prompt shown), pressed.
- Trigger. E, a click, or the touch button, with a 300 ms cooldown, ignored while paused or in a dialogue or story. Emit object:interact with the payload defined in the contract.
- Every InteractionKind and InteractionAction in the contract is handled. inspect, read, use and pickup emit the event (a picked-up object is hidden); talk or an action of type dialogue emits the event and makes the character turn toward the player; an action of type teleport within the same world calls teleportTo, while a target in another world is only emitted, because switching worlds is Foundation’s job; custom is only emitted.
- Characters. Characters are focusable even without an InteractiveObject entry. The agent must check how the contract identifies characters in object:focus and object:interact payloads; if there is no character form, it raises a Contract Change Request instead of inventing one.
5.7 Nearby detection and location tracking
- Runs at 5 Hz on plain data. Produces a snapshot: nearby characters within 6 m, nearby objects within 1.5 times their interactionRadius, and the current location.
- Current location. The location whose centre is within its radius. If several overlap, choose the smallest distance-to-radius ratio. Hysteresis: to leave a location the player must pass radius + 1 m, so standing on the edge does not flicker.
- Events. Emit location:enter and location:exit exactly once per transition. Evaluate the initial location immediately after world:ready, so the HUD is correct before the first step. Leaving every location emits only location:exit.
- The tracker is pure TypeScript with unit tests: enter, exit, overlap, hysteresis, spawn inside a location, teleport.
5.8 Location navigation
- teleportTo(locationId) looks the location up in the loaded world. An unknown id logs a development warning and does nothing.
- Where the player lands. The contract’s Location has a centre position and an optional cameraHint. Use cameraHint.position and face cameraHint.lookAt when present. Otherwise stand at the nearest walkable point to the centre and face the first character or object of that location. If a future contract adds explicit spawn positions, prefer them.
- Sequence. 400 ms fade out, set position and yaw, push out of any collider, reset velocity, emit location:exit for the old location and location:enter for the new one, 400 ms fade in. Teleporting to the current location only repositions.
5.9 Mobile support
- The engine draws its own touch overlay inside its container, with touch-action: none, so Foundation’s pages stay untouched.
- Joystick. Bottom-left, 120 px base and 52 px thumb, 12 percent dead zone, response curve x^1.3.
- Look. Dragging on the right 60 percent of the screen, 0.004 rad per pixel. Touches that start on a button never turn the camera. Track each finger by pointerId so move and look work at the same time.
- Buttons. At least 48×48 px with 8 px spacing and safe-area insets. Sprint (toggle) and Interact (visible only while a target is focused).
- Shown only when needed. Visible on touch devices (coarse pointer or the first touch event) and hidden for mouse users.
- Orientation. Landscape is primary; in portrait show a gentle, non-blocking “rotate your device” hint.
- Mobile quality. Start on the Low or Medium tier when the device has a high pixel ratio and few CPU cores, and let adaptive quality adjust (5.11).
- Audio unlock. The first touch on Enter unlocks audio.
- Foundation request. Pull-to-refresh and page-zoom prevention must be set on the page body, which the engine may not touch. The agent lists this in its Foundation request report instead of editing the page.
5.10 Characters inside the engine
- Placement. The contract gives a character a homeLocationId but no position. Until a contract change adds one (propose it as CCR-9: Character.placement?: SpawnPoint), place the character on a ring of radius 2 m around the location centre, facing the centre, spaced by index.
- Actor. CharacterActor clones the model with SkeletonUtils.clone, applies appearance.scale, and exposes the states idle, talk, walk, sit and gesture:<name>.
- Animation. An AnimationMixer with 0.25 s cross-fades. Clip names come from idleAnimation, talkAnimation and the gesture map (CCR-5). A missing clip falls back to procedural motion: breathing (spine scale ±0.5 percent) and a slow sway.
- Gaze. Within 5 m and inside a ±70° frontal cone the head turns toward the player, limited to ±60° yaw and ±25° pitch and smoothed with damping. Blinking through a morph target every 2–6 s for about 120 ms when the model has one.
- Turning. On dialogue:start the actor rotates toward the player over 0.6 s. Three seconds after dialogue:end it returns to its home facing.
- Speaking. setCharacterSpeaking(id, true) switches to the talk animation. Mouth movement is driven by voice:lip-sync events when they arrive; otherwise a damped procedural value (0.15 + 0.5 × |sin(9t + noise)|) is used. The mouth closes 100 ms after speech ends. It maps to a mouth morph target if the model has one, otherwise to a jaw bone.
- Gestures. The gesture value from the AI turn (greet, explain, emphasize, think, welcome, point, bow, sad-sigh, none) plays once, then returns to idle or talk. A missing gesture clip is skipped silently.
- Crowds. Background people (servants, merchants, students) are instanced low-poly walkers that follow paths, with a per-instance phase offset for walking bob. They are not skinned meshes, so dozens cost almost nothing.
- Placeholders. PlaceholderFigure (capsule body, sphere head, robe cylinder) exists so Phases 2–3 can be tested before real models arrive. It is labelled in the debug overlay, and a ready world may not contain one.
