2. src/engine/core/: ResourceTracker, AssetLoader (decoder files copied into public/), quality tiers, the lifecycle state machine, the renderer implementation, and src/engine/index.ts exporting createWorldRenderer() with the dynamic import.
3. src/engine/scene/: SceneBuilder, the prefab registry with the starter prefabs (wall, floor, column row, arch, dome, fountain, stairs, platform), material presets, instanced scatter, and lighting, sky and fog from the theme. This needs CCR-1 approved first; ask the user, then update the contracts.
4. src/engine/physics/: CollisionWorld with the unit tests listed in 5.5.
5. src/engine/player/ and src/engine/controls/: keyboard, pointer lock with drag fallback, fixed timestep, pause and resume.
6. Test fixture. src/engine/__fixtures__/testWorld.ts builds a valid World named TEST ENVIRONMENT (rooms, walls, a step, a platform, boxes). It is used only by tests and by a route /dev/engine-test that exists only in development builds. It is never shown as a historical world.
7. Connect WorldViewPage to createWorldRenderer() for worlds that have a scene; planned worlds still show the “being built” panel. In a team this edit belongs to Foundation; here the user approves it.
Done when. In /dev/engine-test you can walk with WASD and the mouse; you cannot pass through walls; a small step is climbed and a high one blocks you; you can fall and land; pause and resume work; all CollisionWorld tests pass; calling unmount() twice causes no error; re-mounting leaves exactly one <canvas>.
Phase 3 — Interaction, nearby detection, navigation, mobile (Task 02, parts D–H)
Goal. Everything else Task 02 asks for, plus the baseline performance and leak checks.
Read. Sections 5.6–5.9 and 5.11.
Steps.
1. interaction/: pick volumes, focus system, reticle, and the dispatcher that handles every InteractionKind and InteractionAction, emitting object:focus and object:interact through the shared event bus.
2. nearby/: the nearby detector and the location tracker with hysteresis, emitting location:enter and location:exit.
3. navigation/: teleportTo(locationId) with fades and correct events.
4. controls/: the touch overlay (joystick, look area, sprint, interact).
5. characters/: the CharacterActor shell, PlaceholderFigure, and setCharacterSpeaking.
6. debug/: a development-only overlay with FPS, draw calls, triangles, memory counters, collider view and the nearby snapshot.
7. Write the automated leak test (5.11) and the unit tests for the tracker and nearby detector.
8. Write docs/TESTING.md with a table mapping each of the 13 test items in Task 02 to the exact test or manual step that proves it.
9. Produce the Task 02 final report, including the list of requests for Foundation, without editing Foundation’s files.
Done when. In the test room: the reticle changes on a focusable object, the prompt appears, E fires object:interact once; walking between two location circles fires exactly one enter and one exit; teleportTo repositions with the right events; the touch controls work on a phone (or in the browser’s device emulation); ten load-and-unload cycles leave memory counters unchanged.
Phase 4 — Baghdad environment
Goal. A walkable, lit, data-driven Baghdad palace district.
Read. Section 7.1, Sections 5.3 and 6.4 (for later), 2.10.
Steps.
1. Write docs/SOURCES.md entries for every historical statement you will use, then fill src/data/worlds/baghdad.ts completely: theme, spawn, six locations, objects, character records, the assets[] list and the scene (SceneSpec, per approved CCR-1).
2. Add any prefabs still missing: arcade, iwan, dome, palm, stall, lantern, shelf, dais, gate, simple horse, instanced walkers.
3. Source CC0 or other legally usable textures and models; run scripts/optimize-models.mjs; add a row for each in docs/ASSETS.md and an AssetRef in the world.
4. Build the scene from the coordinates in 7.1, add colliders and bounds, and set the golden lighting and fog.
5. Create scripts/validate-worlds.ts with the rules in 4.7 and the script validate:worlds; run it before build.
6. The status becomes in-progress.
Done when. You can walk gate → courtyard → audience hall → garden → library → market without clipping through walls or getting stuck; the HUD chip changes at each location; validate:worlds passes; the debug overlay reports frame rate and draw calls and you paste those numbers into PROJECT_CONTEXT.md.
Phase 5 — Characters and animation
Goal. Living characters: Harun al-Rashid and the supporting people.
Read. Section 5.10 and the characters of 7.1.
Steps.
1. Choose character models from CC0 sources (for example Quaternius, Kenney, KayKit). If you use Mixamo animations, read its current terms first and record them in docs/ASSETS.md; prefer CC0 animation packs. Do not commit anything whose licence forbids redistribution.
2. Give each character period-appropriate costume using material colours and simple accessories built in code (turban, sash, cap). Do not claim photorealism.
3. Implement CharacterActor fully: idle, talk, walk and gesture clips with cross-fades; procedural fallback; gaze; blinking; turn toward the player; lip-sync driver.
4. Place Harun in the audience hall (standing near the dais if no sitting animation exists, and say so in PROJECT_CONTEXT.md); place the steward, scholar and merchant in their locations; add the instanced guards, servants and grooms.
5. Add a development panel with buttons for each gesture and a talking toggle, so animation can be tested without the AI.
Done when. Approaching Harun shows the reticle and prompt; pressing E makes him turn toward you; every gesture button plays; the talking toggle animates the mouth or jaw; the characters appear in the right locations; no placeholder figure remains in Baghdad.
Phase 6 — AI dialogue (backend and interface)
Goal. A conversation with Harun in Modern Standard Arabic, with fact tags, working with no key and with a key.
Read. Sections 6.1, 6.2, 3.2 (S7), 2.10.
Steps.
1. Scaffold server/ (Express, zod, helmet, cors, express-rate-limit, dotenv, run with tsx), the Vite /api proxy, .env.example, and the dev, dev:web, dev:server scripts.
2. Implement the providers and the scripted provider; implement the prompt builder; write src/data/knowledge/harun-al-rashid.ts with at least 25 grounding facts (each with an id, tag and source reference) and at least 12 mustAvoid items.
3. Write the scripted intents (at least 12) for Harun, with the Arabic normaliser and unit tests.
4. Implement src/ai/ so it satisfies the contract’s AIService.
5. Build the dialogue panel (S7) in src/components/: portrayal notice strip, bubbles, fact chips, suggested questions, history, English translation toggle, close. Wire object:interact (talk) to dialogue:start, and each DialogueTurn to the gesture, setCharacterSpeaking and the text.
6. Server tests for validation, rate limiting and invalid JSON recovery. Unit tests for the prompt builder.
7. Write the manual red-team list into docs/TESTING.md: anachronism questions, requests to break character, prompt-injection attempts, requests for invented quotations, English and colloquial Arabic input.
Done when. With no key, you can hold a scripted conversation and the “offline mode” chip shows; with a key, real replies arrive; every reply is in Modern Standard Arabic with tags; an anachronism question is refused in character; searching the built dist/ folder finds no key; the portrayal notice is always visible.